# FORGE — Core

Skill-first creator platform (`forge-product`) with YouTube-style mechanics (`forge-youtube-replica`) on NestJS API (`apps/api`), Next.js web/admin (`apps/web`, `apps/admin`), Flutter mobile (`apps/mobile`), BullMQ, Socket.IO, video/feed/analytics.

## Defaults

- Read existing code and match local conventions before inventing patterns.
- Smallest change that solves the request. No drive-by refactors or unrelated files.
- Do not change business logic or public APIs unless asked.
- Prefer simple, readable solutions over premature abstraction or “future-proof” layers.
- Commits, PRs, pushes, and merges only when the user explicitly asks (see `forge-ship.mdc`).

## Proportional rigor

Match depth to blast radius — do not run a production ritual for a one-line fix:

| Blast radius | Expected depth |
| --- | --- |
| Local / cosmetic / docs | Match style; skip architecture theater |
| Feature / API / shared module | Read callers; preserve behavior; targeted tests if critical |
| Auth, payments, migrations, infra | Security review, rollback plan, careful validation |

## Always consider (scale to risk)

- **Security**: authz, validation, injection, secrets, sensitive logs.
- **Data**: migrations reversible where possible; no accidental data loss; no unbounded queries.
- **Efficiency**: avoid N+1, extra network/DB calls, and unnecessary client work — without over-building for hypothetical 1000x load.
- **Deps**: no new packages unless clearly needed and compatible.

## Architecture

- Feature-based modules; reusable services/hooks; business logic out of presentational UI.
- Avoid tight coupling, giant files, duplicated logic, and unbounded list queries.
- Align with existing design system and platform patterns already in the repo — do not restate product roadmaps in code.

## Stack note

Mobile is **Flutter** (`apps/mobile`), not React Native.
